    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Most failed DIY seal jobs fail before the first pail is opened: the surface was dusty, the cracks were left open, or the forecast was ignored. Get the prep and the weather right, spread the sealer thin, and a homeowner with a squeegee can get a result that holds up as well as a contractor job for a fraction of the price.
        </p>

        <TopPick />

        <div id="does-it-need-it" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Check Whether the Driveway Actually Needs Sealing
          </h2>

          <p>
            Before buying anything, spend ten minutes looking at the surface. Fresh asphalt is deep black and slightly glossy because the binder on top is still rich in oils. As sunlight oxidizes that binder, the color drifts toward charcoal and then a dusty gray, and the small stones in the mix start to show. When you can see bare aggregate across most of the driveway, the top layer has lost its protection and sealing will help.
          </p>

          <p>
            Next, do a splash check. Pour a cup of water on a few spots on a dry, mild day. If it sits in beads or spreads slowly, the surface is still tight and you can probably wait another season. If it darkens the asphalt and disappears within a minute, water is getting into the pavement, and in a freeze-thaw climate that water will expand inside the pores every cold night. That is the situation sealer is designed to prevent.
          </p>

          <p>
            Age matters too. A driveway paved within the last six to twelve months should not be sealed yet, because the oils that keep it flexible are still working their way out. Our guide on <a href="/when-should-you-seal-a-new-asphalt-driveway/">when to seal a new asphalt driveway</a> covers that waiting period in detail. At the other end, a driveway that is broken into potholes, sinking, or covered in alligator cracking needs repair or resurfacing; sealer only coats the top and will not hold failing pavement together.
          </p>

          <p>
            If the driveway passes these checks, plan on a two-day job for a typical two-car driveway: one day for cleaning and crack repair, one day for coating. Many people split it across a weekend with a full day of drying in between. Pick a stretch where the forecast shows dry weather and daytime temperatures above 50°F for at least three days in a row, which gives you room for prep, application, and the first night of curing.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="shopping-list" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Shopping List and How Much Sealer to Buy
          </h2>

          <p>
            Measure the driveway length and width with a tape and multiply. A straight single-car driveway of 10 by 50 feet is 500 square feet; a two-car driveway of 20 by 40 feet is 800. Add any parking pad or turnaround separately. Then check the coverage figure printed on the pail you plan to buy. Most water-based asphalt emulsion sealers list somewhere around 250 to 400 square feet per 5-gallon pail per coat, depending on how porous the surface is.
          </p>

          <p>
            Use the low end of that range for an old, rough, gray driveway, because open texture soaks up more material. Two coats are the norm, so a 800-square-foot driveway at 300 square feet per pail needs roughly six pails in total. Buy one extra and keep the receipt; running out halfway down the driveway on a hot afternoon leaves a visible lap mark where the second batch meets the first. Unopened pails can usually be returned.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Item</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Why You Need It</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical Cost</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Asphalt sealer, 5-gal pails</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">The coating itself, two coats</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Roughly $25-$60 per pail</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Pourable or caulk-tube crack filler</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Seals cracks up to about 1/2 inch</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Roughly $10-$25 per jug</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Squeegee/brush combo tool</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Spreads sealer in thin, even passes</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Roughly $15-$35</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Stiff push broom and leaf blower</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Removes grit and dust before coating</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Often already owned</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Oil spot primer</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Lets sealer bond over drip stains</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Roughly $10-$20</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Edging brush and painter's tape</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Clean lines along concrete and siding</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Roughly $10</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Drill mixer paddle</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Restirs settled solids in each pail</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Roughly $10-$20</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            For the sealer itself, the choice that matters most is the base. Water-based asphalt emulsion and acrylic-modified products are the mainstream DIY options, clean up with water, and are legal everywhere. Coal-tar products are tougher against gasoline and oil but are banned in several states and many municipalities because of their polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbon content. Our ranked list of the <a href="/best-asphalt-driveway-sealer-reviews/">best asphalt driveway sealers</a> sorts the current options by budget and driveway condition.
          </p>

          <p>
            Wear old clothes, rubber boots or shoes you will throw away, and chemical-resistant gloves. Sealer does not come out of fabric, and it tracks easily on shoe soles. If you are working near a light-colored garage floor or walkway, a roll of cheap cardboard or a drop cloth to stand on at the transitions saves a lot of scrubbing later.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cleaning" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Day One: Clean the Surface and Let It Dry
          </h2>

          <p>
            Sealer bonds to asphalt, not to dust, pollen, sand, or loose stones. Start by trimming grass and weeds back from the edges with a string trimmer or edger so the full width of the pavement is exposed. Pull any weeds growing in cracks, roots included, or they will push up through the patch within weeks. Then sweep the whole driveway with a stiff broom and follow with a leaf blower to pull fine dust out of the texture.
          </p>

          <p>
            Wash the surface next. A garden hose with a jet nozzle and a stiff brush works for a driveway in decent shape. A pressure washer is faster, but keep the tip moving and do not use a narrow zero-degree nozzle, which can strip binder and dislodge stones from older asphalt. Scrub any oil or transmission fluid stains with a degreaser made for driveways and rinse thoroughly, since sealer will not stick to a greasy film.
          </p>

          <p>
            Once dry, coat each oil stain that remains with an oil spot primer. These products seal the residue so the water-based sealer can bond over it instead of beading up and peeling off. Spots that are still soft or tacky after degreasing may need a second cleaning; primer cannot fix asphalt that has been softened by a heavy, long-running leak, and those patches may need to be cut out and filled.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE DRY-SURFACE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Wait until the pavement is dry to the touch everywhere, including the shady side.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">After washing, most driveways need at least 24 hours of dry weather before coating, and shaded or low spots can take longer. Press a paper towel into the darkest-looking areas; if it comes up damp, wait. Sealer laid over trapped moisture is the most common reason for patches that peel within the first year.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Use the drying time productively. Mask the bottom of garage door trim, the edges of concrete walkways, and any brick or stone borders with painter's tape. Lay cardboard over the concrete apron at the top of the driveway if it meets the garage. Park both cars on the street or a neighbor's driveway the night before day two, and tell household members which door to use so nobody walks across the finished surface.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="crack-repair" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Day One: Fill Cracks and Patch Low Spots
          </h2>

          <p>
            Cracks are where water gets underneath the pavement, so filling them matters more than the coating that goes over them. Blow or vacuum debris out of each crack and make sure it is dry. Hairline cracks narrower than about 1/8 inch can be bridged by sealer alone, especially a product with sand or rubber in it. Anything wider needs a dedicated crack filler before you coat.
          </p>

          <p>
            For cracks from roughly 1/8 to 1/2 inch wide, a pourable rubberized filler in a squeeze jug or a caulk-gun tube is the easiest choice. Fill slightly below the surface rather than mounding it up, and smooth with a putty knife or the squeegee end of your tool. For cracks deeper than about half an inch, push foam backer rod into the gap first so the filler does not disappear into the base. Our roundup of the <a href="/best-driveway-crack-filler/">best driveway crack fillers</a> compares the main types.
          </p>

          <p>
            Potholes and broken edges need a cold-patch asphalt repair mix instead. Dig out loose material, fill the hole in layers no thicker than about two inches, and compact each layer hard with a hand tamper or by driving over it with a board on top. Overfill slightly so the patch ends flush after compaction. Cold patch is a stopgap compared with a hot-mix repair, but it is adequate for small holes in a residential driveway.
          </p>

          <p>
            Crack fillers and patches need time before being coated. Check the label on the filler you buy; many water-based fillers want several hours to a full day before sealing, and some rubberized products cure faster in warm sun. Doing repairs on day one and coating on day two builds in that wait naturally. If a filler has shrunk into the crack overnight, add a second pass and let it set before you start the sealer.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="first-coat" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Day Two: Lay Down the First Thin Coat
          </h2>

          <p>
            Start in the morning once the dew has burned off and the pavement is dry. Check the label temperature limits; most asphalt sealers call for air and surface temperatures of at least 50°F and rising, with no rain expected for 24 hours or more. Very hot afternoons are also a problem because sealer can skin over before it bonds. On sunny summer days, a light mist of water on the pavement just ahead of the squeegee can slow flash drying, but never leave puddles.
          </p>

          <p>
            Mix every pail thoroughly. Sand and filler settle to the bottom during storage, and a pail that is stirred only at the top leaves the first half thin and the last half lumpy. A mixer paddle on a drill for two or three minutes is the reliable method. Then cut in the edges with a brush: a strip four to six inches wide along the garage apron, walkways, and the street edge, so the squeegee never has to come close to those borders.
          </p>

          <p>
            Work from the top of the driveway toward the street so you never have to walk back across wet sealer. Pour a ribbon of sealer across the width in a section about three or four feet deep, then pull it with the squeegee in long, overlapping passes, finishing in one direction so the texture lines up. The goal is a thin, even film that you can still see the surface texture through. If it puddles in low spots, push the excess onto the next section.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Typical Label Conditions</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">50°F+</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Air and surface temp, rising</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">24 hr</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Dry forecast after coating</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">2</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Thin coats beat one thick coat</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">250-400</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Sq ft per 5-gal pail, per coat</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Keep a wet edge. If you stop for lunch halfway, end at a straight line across the driveway rather than in a ragged patch, and start the next section by overlapping only slightly. Thick ridges at overlaps dry slower and look darker. When the pail is half empty, restir it, because solids continue to settle as you work. A long-handled squeegee lets you stand on dry pavement downhill of the coat rather than on fresh sealer.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="second-coat" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Second Coat, Then Let It Cure
          </h2>

          <p>
            The second coat goes on once the first is dry enough to walk on without leaving prints, which on a warm, sunny day is often four to eight hours. Read your product label, because some formulas want longer and cool or humid weather stretches every figure. Apply it the same way as the first, but pull the squeegee at a right angle to the first pass. Crossing directions fills in thin streaks and gives a more uniform color.
          </p>

          <p>
            Two thin coats outperform one heavy coat for a simple reason: thin films release their water evenly and bond all the way through. A thick coat skins on top while the bottom stays soft, which leads to tire marks, scuffing, and in the worst cases a layer that peels off in sheets. If the second coat is going to run into evening, it is better to wait and do it the next morning than to lay it down as temperatures fall.
          </p>

          <p>
            Block the driveway with sawhorses, trash cans, or caution tape across the street end. Foot traffic is generally fine after the surface is dry to the touch, but cars need longer: most labels call for 24 to 48 hours before driving on it, and longer in cool weather. Hot tires turning on soft sealer leave scuff marks, so for the first week try to roll straight in and out without turning the wheels while the car is stopped.
          </p>

          <p>
            Timing ranges are wide because temperature, humidity, sun, and film thickness all change how quickly water leaves the coating. Our breakdown of <a href="/how-long-for-driveway-sealer-to-dry/">how long driveway sealer takes to dry</a> covers each stage, and if a storm shows up early, see what to do about <a href="/rain-after-driveway-sealing/">rain after driveway sealing</a>. When in doubt, give it an extra day before parking.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cleanup" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Cleanup, Disposal, and Fixing Problem Spots
          </h2>

          <p>
            Water-based sealer cleans up with water while it is still wet. Rinse the squeegee, brushes, and mixing paddle immediately, ideally into a bucket or onto grass rather than into a storm drain. Sealer that dries on tools is very hard to remove, so do this before you take a break. Pull painter's tape while the edge is still slightly soft to get a clean line; if you wait until it hardens, the tape can lift sealer with it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Drips on concrete or brick come off far more easily when fresh. Wipe them with a damp rag right away. For dried splatter, a commercial asphalt remover or a citrus-based cleaner and a stiff brush usually works, followed by a rinse. Leftover sealer in a sealed pail keeps for a while if it does not freeze, and is handy for touch-ups. Empty pails should be dried out completely before going in the trash; check local rules for liquid disposal.
          </p>

          <p>
            Look over the driveway the following day. Thin spots show as lighter patches and can be touched up with a brush once the main coats are dry. Lap marks and tire scuffs usually soften in appearance after a few weeks of sun. Peeling in a specific area almost always traces back to a damp, dusty, or oily surface under that spot; scrape off the loose material, clean, prime if needed, and recoat that section.
          </p>

          <p>
            Once sealed, a driveway generally needs another coat every two to three years, not every year. Sealing too often builds up layers that crack and flake. Our guide on <a href="/how-often-should-you-seal-your-driveway/">how often to seal a driveway</a> explains how to judge the interval, and if you would rather hand the job off, the numbers in <a href="/how-much-does-it-cost-to-seal-a-driveway/">what it costs to seal a driveway</a> show what a contractor typically charges versus doing it yourself.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Coating Over Dust and Grit</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A quick sweep leaves a fine film of sand and dust in the texture, and sealer bonds to that film instead of the asphalt. The result looks fine for a month and then peels in flakes. Sweep, blow, wash, and let the surface dry fully. The extra hour of cleaning is the cheapest insurance on the whole project.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Pouring It On Thick</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                It is tempting to finish in one heavy coat. A thick layer skins on top and stays soft underneath, so it scuffs under tires and can peel in sheets. Two thin coats at the label coverage rate dry evenly, bond better, and end up with a more uniform color for the same amount of material.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the Crack Filler</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Sealer bridges hairline cracks, but anything wider will open right back up after the first winter. Water then gets under the pavement and the crack grows. Fill every crack wider than about 1/8 inch with a proper filler and let it set before coating, or the money spent on sealer protects the wrong part of the driveway.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting a Sunny Morning Forecast</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Clear skies at breakfast do not mean a dry 24 hours. A pop-up afternoon storm can wash fresh sealer into the street. Check the hourly forecast for the full day and the following night, and confirm the overnight low stays above the label minimum. If the window is marginal, wait for a better one.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Driving On It the Next Morning</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The surface can look dry and still be soft underneath, especially after a cool night. Driving on it too early leaves tire marks and scuffs that stay visible. Give it at least the time the label specifies, usually 24 to 48 hours, and longer when temperatures drop or humidity stays high.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does it take to seal a driveway yourself?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For a typical two-car driveway of about 600 to 900 square feet, plan on most of two days. The first day goes to trimming edges, cleaning, treating oil spots, and filling cracks. The second day covers both coats of sealer, which takes a few hours each including the wait between them. Then add 24 to 48 hours before driving on it, depending on weather and the product label.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I use a squeegee or a brush to apply sealer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A squeegee is the better tool for most driveways because it spreads sealer quickly in a thin, even film and pushes it into the texture. A brush is useful for cutting in edges and for very rough, porous surfaces where you want to work material into the pores. Many combination tools have a squeegee on one side and a brush on the other, which covers both jobs.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I seal my driveway in the fall?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, as long as temperatures stay above the label minimum, usually 50°F, during application and for the first day or two afterward. Early fall is often a good window because days are dry and not scorching. Late fall is riskier because nights get cold and dew lingers, which slows curing. Our guide to the best time to seal a driveway covers seasonal timing in detail.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need to pressure wash before sealing?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not necessarily. A garden hose with a jet nozzle, a stiff brush, and a degreaser for oil spots cleans most driveways well enough. A pressure washer is faster and helps on heavily soiled surfaces, but use a wide fan tip and keep it moving to avoid stripping binder from older asphalt. Whichever method you use, the surface must dry completely before you coat it.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many coats of driveway sealer should I apply?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Two thin coats is the standard recommendation on most asphalt sealer labels. The first coat soaks into the surface and fills texture; the second evens out the color and builds the protective film. A driveway that was sealed recently and is still in good shape can sometimes get by with a single maintenance coat, but a first-time or neglected driveway needs two.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What happens if it rains right after I seal the driveway?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                If rain falls within the first few hours, it can wash uncured sealer off the surface, leave streaks, or turn the finish cloudy. Once the coating has had several hours of dry, warm weather, light rain usually does little harm. If a storm does damage the finish, let everything dry, sweep up any loose material, and recoat the affected areas on the next dry day.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it cheaper to seal a driveway myself?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually, yes. Materials for a typical driveway often come to roughly $100 to $300 including sealer, crack filler, and a squeegee, while contractors commonly charge several hundred dollars for the same space. The trade-off is time and effort. For very large or badly cracked driveways, a contractor with spray equipment and hot crack sealant can be worth the extra cost.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

